## Setting the number of cores used for loading[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/best-practices/loading-resources-in-parallel.html#setting-the-number-of-cores-used-for-loading "Link to this heading")
In the application code, either using the application framework configuration or the resource manager API, you can set the number of cores you want your application to use to load the GPU resources.
To set the number of cores your application uses for loading the resources, in the application code in the `Application::onConfigure[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a11370.html#a3803fce5a93d4211e67cc9be1d14f92c)` function:

```
// Set loadingThreadCount to the number of cores in the processor
// of your target device.
// For example, to use all four cores of a four-core processor,
// set the value to 3, to use only two cores, set the value to 1.
configuration.loadingThreadCount = 3;

```
Copy to clipboard
You can set the loading preferences for your application in the application code in the `Application::onConfigure[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a11370.html#a3803fce5a93d4211e67cc9be1d14f92c)` function or in the `application.cfg`. See [LoadingThreadCount](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/application-configurations/reference-for-application-configuration.html#loadingthreadcount).
## Disabling parallel loading[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/best-practices/loading-resources-in-parallel.html#disabling-parallel-loading "Link to this heading")
To disable the parallel loading of resources, set `ApplicationProperties::loadingThreadCount[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a11374.html#a658dc5228f9d70155baa1e13595034ad)` to 0. When you disable the parallel loading, Kanzi uses only the main thread to load the resources. When you disable the parallel loading, Kanzi does not allocate the memory manager.

```
configuration.loadingThreadCount = 0;

```
Copy to clipboard
See [LoadingThreadCount](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/application-configurations/reference-for-application-configuration.html#loadingthreadcount).

## Decreasing the precision of a fragment shader[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/best-practices/shaders/optimizing-pixel-shaders.html#decreasing-the-precision-of-a-fragment-shader "Link to this heading")
If fragment shading is a performance bottleneck, you can decrease the precision of the shader from full 32-bit floating point to 16-bit floating point. Many GPUs support operations at double rate when you decrease the precision.
To decrease the precision of a fragment shader:
  1. In the Library, select Resource Files > Shaders and open the fragment shader whose precision you want to decrease.
The Shader Source Editor opens.
  2. Declare variables with the appropriate precision qualifier:  
| Precision qualifier  | Range  | Data type  | Examples of use  |  
| --- | --- | --- | --- |  
| `lowp`  |  (−2,2)  |  8-bit decimal. Modern GPUs do not have fixed point shader units and map this to 16-bit floating point. When you are not sure which precision to choose, prefer `mediump` over `lowp`.  | Colors in the RGB data range [0..1] and intensities in the range [0..1].  |  
| `mediump`  |  (−215,215)  |  16-bit floating point with 1 sign bit, 5 bits of exponent, and 10 bits of mantissa. Some GPUs map this to 32-bit floating point.  | Colors, normals, textures, positions in object space.  |  
| `highp`  |  (−2128,2128)  | 32-bit floating point with 1 sign bit, 8 bits of exponent, and 23 bits of mantissa.  | Matrices, large textures, positions in world space.  |  
For example, for a fragment shader, use this code:

```
#version 310 es

// lowp and mediump often double the performance compared to highp.
precision lowp float;

uniform sampler2D Texture;
uniform lowp float BlendIntensity;
in mediump vec2 vTexCoord;
layout(location = 0) out lowp vec4 fragColor;

void main()
{
    vec4 color = texture(Texture, vTexCoord);
    fragColor = color * BlendIntensity;
}

```
Copy to clipboard
Do not use this code:

```
#version 310 es

// Default to highp for everything.
precision highp float;
uniform sampler2D Texture;
uniform float BlendIntensity;
in vec2 vTexCoord;
layout(location = 0) out vec4 fragColor;

void main()
{
    vec4 color = texture(Texture, vTexCoord);
    fragColor = color * BlendIntensity;
}

```
