### Multiple owning references[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/java/using-java.html#multiple-owning-references "Link to this heading")
When `ObjectRef[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-java-api/a02659.html)` instances are passed between blocks of code, it can become unclear when to close an instance. Because Java passes instances by reference, what appears to be independent instances, could all refer to the same strong reference. To avoid these issues, Kanzi uses the following guidelines with `ObjectRef[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-java-api/a02659.html)` instances and recommends the users to do the same:
  * When returning an `ObjectRef[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-java-api/a02659.html)` instance as a return value from a method, the ownership is passed with the instance. Methods must either return a clone of the instance, or an unclosed local-only reference. This also means that `ObjectRef[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-java-api/a02659.html)` instances that are received from a method return, are owned by the calling code and can be closed when no longer needed or left for garbage collection.
Java

```
// When returning a member ObjectRef, return a clone.
ObjectRef<EmptyNode2D> getNode1()
{
    return mNode.clone();
}

// You can return a local ObjectRef directly, but do not close it explicitly.
ObjectRef<EmptyNode2D> getNode2()
{
    ObjectRef<EmptyNode2D> localRef = EmptyNode2D.create(getDomain(), "My Empty Node");
    return localRef;

    // Here, do not close the localRef.
}

```
Copy to clipboard
Kotlin

```
// When returning a member ObjectRef, return a clone.
fun getNode1(): ObjectRef<EmptyNode2D> {
    return mNode.clone()
}

// You can return a local ObjectRef directly, but do not close it explicitly.
fun getNode2(): ObjectRef<EmptyNode2D> {
    var localRef = EmptyNode2D.create(domain, "My Empty Node")

    return localRef;

    // Here, do not close the localRef.
}

