## Exceptions[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/java/using-java.html#exceptions "Link to this heading")
Kanzi uses checked exceptions to indicate recoverable errors. For example, if the resource is not found when you call the `Node.acquireResource[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-java-api/a02615.html#a5956cb64994c16db7d1cb72af51c5f72)` method, Kanzi throws the `ObjectNotFoundException[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-java-api/a02655.html)` exception.
Kanzi Engine exceptions thrown by native code are packaged into instances of `NativeException`. It is not expected for Java code to handle these exceptions. Instead, allow the exceptions to propagate up the stack so that Kanzi retranslates them to a native exceptions that Kanzi Engine can handle.
## Using custom JNI extensions[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/java/using-java.html#using-custom-jni-extensions "Link to this heading")
You can implement custom JNI code to access areas of Kanzi Engine C++ API not yet covered by Kanzi Java API. To ease integration with JNI code, all relevant Kanzi Java API classes come with method `long getNative()`, which returns a pointer to their C++ counterpart. For the exact C++ type of the pointer, see the API reference for respective Kanzi Java API class.

## Migrating application code[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/android/droidfw/migrating-from-appfw.html#migrating-application-code "Link to this heading")
After you migrate an application from Kanzi application framework (appfw) to Kanzi Android framework (droidfw), the C++ code in that application is no longer in use. You can migrate the existing C++ code of the application to Java application or plugin code, or refactor the functionality to a C++ Kanzi Engine plugin. Java API does not implement the complete Kanzi Engine API and therefore the approach to migrating the application code depends on the functionality of your application.
You can remove all except one cpp file. To build an application, CMake requires one cpp file, even if that file is empty. For example, remove the content of the `Application/src/coin.cpp`.
For example, to use Java to show in the Coin example the frame time in a Text Block node:
  1. In the `app/src/main/java/com/rightware/kanzi/coin/Coin.java`, in the application activity, add a method to the `Coin` class:

```
void updateFrameTime(Duration duration)
{
    TextBlock2D frameTime = mView.getRoot().lookupNode("#FrameTime");
    frameTime.setText("Frame time: " + duration.toMillis() + " ms");
}

```
Copy to clipboard
  2. In `onCreate(Bundle icicle)`, add a main loop scheduler task to execute the method every frame:

```
KanziRuntime.Reference runtimeRef = KanziRuntime.acquire(this);

mView.addListener(new KanziViewListener() {
     @Override
     public void onStartupPrefabLoaded(View view, Domain domain) {
         runtimeRef.get().getDomain().getMainLoopScheduler().appendTask(
                 MainLoopScheduler.UserStage,
                 "Update Frame Time",
                 TaskRecurrence.Recurring, duration -> updateFrameTime(duration));
     }
});

```
