To learn more about creating Kanzi Engine plugins with Java or Kotlin, see [Creating a Java Kanzi Engine plugin using a template](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/creating-kanzi-engine-plugins.html#creating-java-kanzi-engine-plugin-using-template) and [Creating a Java Kanzi Engine plugin manually](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/creating-kanzi-engine-plugins.html#creating-java-kanzi-engine-plugin-manually).

## Platform-specific code[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/java/using-java.html#platform-specific-code "Link to this heading")
Java plugins can run on multiple platforms, such as Kanzi Android framework (droidfw) and in the Kanzi Studio Preview. Make sure that the plugin runs platform-specific code only when it is running on a compatible platform. For example, call the Android API only when the plugin is running in an Android environment.
To detect the environment, use the `Platform[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-java-api/a02663.html)` methods:
Java

```
if (Platform.isAndroid())
{
    // Initialize the code path for when the plugin runs on Android.
    Object context = domain.getPlatformContext();
}
else
{
    // Initialize the code path for when the plugin runs in the Kanzi Studio Preview.
}

```
Copy to clipboard
Kotlin

```
if (Platform.isAndroid()) {
    // Initialize the code path for when the plugin runs on Android.
    val context = domain.platformContext
} else {
    // Initialize the code path for when the plugin runs in the Kanzi Studio Preview.
}

```
Copy to clipboard
## Exceptions[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/java/using-java.html#exceptions "Link to this heading")
Kanzi uses checked exceptions to indicate recoverable errors. For example, if the resource is not found when you call the `Node.acquireResource[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-java-api/a02615.html#a5956cb64994c16db7d1cb72af51c5f72)` method, Kanzi throws the `ObjectNotFoundException[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-java-api/a02655.html)` exception.
Kanzi Engine exceptions thrown by native code are packaged into instances of `NativeException`. It is not expected for Java code to handle these exceptions. Instead, allow the exceptions to propagate up the stack so that Kanzi retranslates them to a native exceptions that Kanzi Engine can handle.
## Using custom JNI extensions[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/java/using-java.html#using-custom-jni-extensions "Link to this heading")
You can implement custom JNI code to access areas of Kanzi Engine C++ API not yet covered by Kanzi Java API. To ease integration with JNI code, all relevant Kanzi Java API classes come with method `long getNative()`, which returns a pointer to their C++ counterpart. For the exact C++ type of the pointer, see the API reference for respective Kanzi Java API class.

## Using the Flow Layout 3D node in the API[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/layouts/using-flow-layouts.html#using-the-flow-layout-3d-node-in-the-api "Link to this heading")
To create a Flow Layout 3D node:

```
// Create a Flow Layout 3D node named Flow. The default primary direction is
// left-to-right and the secondary direction top-to-bottom.
FlowLayout3DSharedPtr flow = FlowLayout3D::create(domain, "Flow");

```
Copy to clipboard
To set the width of a Flow Layout 3D node:

```
// Set the width of the Flow Layout 3D node to 6 device-independent pixels to
// limit its size in its primary direction. By limiting the size in
// the primary direction you force the node to create a new line in
// the secondary direction, when it cannot fit an item in the primary direction.
flow->setWidth(6.0f);

```
Copy to clipboard
To add items to a Flow Layout 3D node:

```
// Flow Layout 3D node arranges its items in the order that you add them.
// Create a 3D node ...
Model3DSharedPtr item1 = Model3D::createBox(domain, "item1", Vector3(2.0f, 1.0f, 1.0f), ThemeRed);
// ... and add it to the Flow Layout 3D node.
flow->addChild(item1);
// The second item still fits in the first line.
Model3DSharedPtr item2 = Model3D::createBox(domain, "item2", Vector3(3.0f, 1.0f, 1.0f), ThemeGreen);
flow->addChild(item2);
// There is still space in the first line, but the third item is
// too large to fit, so the Flow Layout 3D node creates a new line.
Model3DSharedPtr item3 = Model3D::createBox(domain, "item3", Vector3(5.0f, 1.0f, 1.0f), ThemeBlue);
flow->addChild(item3);
// The fourth item fits in the second line by leaving no empty space.
Model3DSharedPtr item4 = Model3D::createBox(domain, "item4", Vector3(1.0f, 1.0f, 1.0f), ThemeOrange);
flow->addChild(item4);
// The Flow Layout 3D node places the last item in a new line.
Model3DSharedPtr item5 = Model3D::createBox(domain, "item5", Vector3(3.0f, 1.0f, 1.0f), ThemeGreen);
flow->addChild(item5);

```
Copy to clipboard
For details, see the `FlowLayout3D[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a18666.html)` class.

