## Creating your own smart material[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/materials/using-smart-materials.html#creating-your-own-smart-material "Link to this heading")
Here you use material rules to create your own smart material that enables rendering either a color or a texture, or both.
To create your own smart material:
  1. In the Library > Materials and Textures > Material Types, create or select the material type for which you want to create a material rule.
For example, create a custom material type that supports rendering either a color or a texture, or both:
    1. In the Library, press `Alt` and right-click Materials and Textures, select Material Type, and name it SmartColorTexture.
![../../_images/create-material-type.png](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/_images/create-material-type.png) ![../../_images/library-smart-color-texture.png](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/_images/library-smart-color-texture.png)
    2. In the Library, expand the SmartColorTexture material type and double-click the Vertex Shader to open it in the Shader Source Editor. Replace the contents of the shader file with

```
#version 310 es

precision mediump float;

in vec3 kzPosition;
uniform highp mat4 kzProjectionCameraWorldMatrix;

#if USE_TEXTURE
in vec2 kzTextureCoordinate0;
uniform vec2 TextureOffset;
uniform vec2 TextureTiling;
out vec2 vTexCoord;
#endif

void main()
{
#if USE_TEXTURE
    vTexCoord = kzTextureCoordinate0 * TextureTiling + TextureOffset;
#endif
    gl_Position = kzProjectionCameraWorldMatrix * vec4(kzPosition.xyz, 1.0);
}

```
Copy to clipboard
Click Save.
    3. Double-click the SmartColorTexture > Fragment Shader to open it in the Shader Source Editor. Replace the contents of the shader file with

```
#version 310 es

precision mediump float;

uniform float BlendIntensity;

#if USE_COLOR
uniform lowp vec4 Ambient;
#endif

#if USE_TEXTURE
in vec2 vTexCoord;
uniform sampler2D Texture;
#endif

layout(location = 0) out vec4 fragColor;

void main()
{

#if USE_TEXTURE
    vec4 textureColor = texture(Texture, vTexCoord);
#endif

#if USE_COLOR && USE_TEXTURE
    float alpha = Ambient.a * BlendIntensity;

    fragColor.rgb = textureColor.rgb * Ambient.rgb * alpha;
    fragColor.a   = textureColor.a * alpha;

#elif USE_COLOR
    fragColor = Ambient * BlendIntensity;

#elif USE_TEXTURE
    fragColor = textureColor * BlendIntensity;

#else
    fragColor = vec4(0.0);
#endif

}

For example:
  1. In the Library > Materials and Textures > Material Types duplicate the DefaultBlit material type, and rename the new material type and the material which uses that material type.
Tip
If your project does not contain the DefaultBlit material type, in the Library > Materials and Textures press `Alt` and right-click Material Types, and select DefaultBlit.
![../../_images/duplicate-defaultblit-material-type.png](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/_images/duplicate-defaultblit-material-type.png) ![../../_images/non-premultiplied-material-type.png](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/_images/non-premultiplied-material-type.png)
  2. In the Library > Materials and Textures > Material Types double-click the Fragment Shader of the new material type to open it in the Shader Source Editor, and replace the content of the fragment shader file with:

```
#version 310 es

precision lowp float;

uniform sampler2D ContentTexture;
in mediump vec2 vTexCoord;
layout(location = 0) out vec4 fragColor;

void main()
{
    vec4 color = texture(ContentTexture, vTexCoord);

    if(color.a > 0.0)
    {
        color.rgb /= color.a;
    }

    fragColor = color;
}

```
Copy to clipboard
  3. In the Library > Materials and Textures > Material Types select the material type whose fragment shader you edited, in the Properties click Sync with Uniforms, and in the Delete Property Type dialog click Yes to delete the bindings and property types that the material type no longer uses.
![../../_images/non-premultiplied-sync-with-uniforms.png](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/_images/non-premultiplied-sync-with-uniforms.png)
  4. In the Library > Materials and Textures > Brushes create a Material Brush and in the Properties set the Material property to the material which uses the material type that you created.
![../../_images/create-material-brush.png](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/_images/create-material-brush.png) ![../../_images/non-premultiplied-brush.png](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/_images/non-premultiplied-brush.png) ![../../_images/non-premultiplied-brush-properties.png](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/_images/non-premultiplied-brush-properties.png)
